analysisWe need to produce assert statement for the comment.

Comment: 
// @@@ If the index is between 0 and the initial size of the list inclusive, then if the element is null, the element at that index is also null, or the element at that index is equal to the inserted element.

Interpretation: Preconditions: index between 0 and initial size inclusive. "initial size" means size before insertion, i.e., old size. So condition: 0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size())? Actually size() method returns size. Use \old(this.size()).

Then postcondition: after addition, at that index, the element is either null (if inserted element is null) OR equals inserted element. Actually phrase: "if the element is null, the element at that index is also null, or the element at that index is equal to the inserted element." Could be: (element == null => this.get(index) == null) && (element != null => this.get(index).equals(element)). But they phrase "or" maybe inclusive: either null case or equal case. So we can express as: (element == null ? this.get(index) == null : this.get(index).equals(element)). Or using implication: (element == null => this.get(index) == null) && (element != null => this.get(index).equals(element)). Simpler: (element == null ? this.get(index) == null : this.get(index).equals(element)). Use Java assert syntax? In spec language, we can write: (element == null => this.get(index) == null) && (element != null => this.get(index).equals(element)). Use &&.

Thus overall assert: (0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size())) => ((element == null => this.get(index) == null) && (element != null => this.get(index).equals(element)));

